reference_answer:
Single Super Phosphate, or SSP, is the most popular phosphatic fertilizer after DAP, as it contains 3 major plant nutrients, namely phosphorus, sulphur, and calcium, along with traces of many micronutrients. SSP is preferred for oilseed crops, as sulfur is an integral part of many edible oils. SSP is the only sulfur-providing fertilizer in India that contains water-soluble phosphorus easily taken up by the plants.

Granulated SSP contains moisture—12.0%;

Available Phosphorus—16.0%;

Water-soluble phosphorus—14.5%;

Sulphur- 11.0%;

Free phosphoric acid: 4.0%.

The recommended dosage should be based on the analysis report of the soil test, which can be accessed from the nearest KVK or soil testing lab. SSP should always be applied as a basal dose (at the time of sowing) and drilled below the seed.
